.TH GGE\-DRAWING 7 "generalGameEngine" "Engine Manual" "Engine Manual"
.SH NAME
gge\-drawing \- primitives, logical pixels, draw order, layers, and smooth motion
.SH DESCRIPTION
All drawing happens inside a scene's \fBrender\fR callback, in \fBlogical pixels\fR (EngineConfig.logical_w x logical_h; the window is scaled to fit and letterboxed). Later calls paint over earlier ones; there is no depth buffer and no retained scene graph. Every visible thing is one of the calls in draw.h, or a module that ends in one of them (sprites, text, tilemaps, menus, dialog).
.PP
.SH THE PRIMITIVES (draw.h)
.TP
\fBdraw_clear(c)\fR
Fill the frame. The engine already clears to EngineConfig.clear_color; use this only to override for one scene.
.TP
\fBdraw_rect(r, c)\fR
Filled rectangle. Placeholders, bars, panels, fades.
.TP
\fBdraw_rect_outline(r, c)\fR
One\-pixel outline.
.TP
\fBdraw_line(x1, y1, x2, y2, c)\fR
One\-pixel line.
.TP
\fBdraw_point(x, y, c)\fR
One pixel.
.TP
\fBdraw_texture(t, src, dst)\fR
Copy a texture region into a rectangle, scaling to fit; src NULL = whole texture.
.TP
\fBdraw_texture_ex(t, src, dst, angle, flip)\fR
The same with rotation (degrees, clockwise, about dst's center) and mirroring.
.TP
\fBdraw_debug_text(x, y, c, s)\fR
Text in the built\-in 8x8 font, always available.
Colors are \fBColor\fR {r,g,b,a} built with rgb(3), rgba(3) or the COLOR_* constants; rectangles are \fBRect\fR {x,y,w,h} built with rect(3), rect_at(3) or rect_centered(3) (gge\-types(7)). Alpha blending is always on: \fBrgba(0, 0, 0, 160)\fR over the whole screen dims whatever was drawn beneath, which is the pause\-menu backdrop and the fade\-to\-black in one call.
.PP
.SH A COMPLETE RENDER
.PP
.RS 4
.EX
static void render(Engine *e, Scene *s, float alpha) {
  (void)s;
  GameState *g = e\->user;
  /* 1. world, through the camera */
  tilemap_draw(&map, &cam);                                   /* background layer */
  for (int i = 0; i < MAX_COINS; ++i) if (g\->coins[i].alive) {
    Vec2 p = camera_to_screen(&cam, g\->coins[i].pos);
    sprite_draw(&coin, (e\->frame / 8) % 4, p.x, p.y, 0, FLIP_NONE);
  }
  Vec2 pp = camera_to_screen(&cam, v2_lerp(prev_player, g\->player.pos, alpha));
  sprite_draw(&hero, anim_frame(&g\->player.anim, &hero_anims[g\->player.anim.anim]),
              pp.x, pp.y, 0, g\->player.facing < 0 ? FLIP_H : FLIP_NONE);
  tilemap_draw(&fg_map, &cam);                                /* foreground layer over entities */
  /* 2. HUD, in screen space */
  draw_rect(rect(8, 8, 100, 8), rgba(0, 0, 0, 120));
  draw_rect(rect(8, 8, 100.0f * g\->player.hp / g\->player.max_hp, 8), COLOR_RED);
  char buf[32]; snprintf(buf, sizeof buf, "%d", g\->player.coins);
  text_draw(ui, 8, 20, COLOR_YELLOW, buf);
  /* 3. debug */
  debug_rect(camera_rect(&cam, player_hitbox(&g\->player)), COLOR_GREEN);   /* F3 only */
}
.EE
.RE
.PP
Order is the layer list: background, world objects sorted however you like, foreground, HUD, debug. Anything that must be on top is simply drawn last.
.PP
.SH SORTING BY DEPTH
For top\-down games where a character can stand in front of or behind a tree, draw world objects in order of their bottom edge (\fBpos.y + h\fR): collect the live entities' indices into a small array each frame, sort by that key (insertion sort is fine for a few hundred), then draw. Doing it in render keeps GameState untouched.
.PP
.SH SMOOTH MOTION (alpha)
\fBupdate\fR runs at a fixed rate; \fBrender\fR runs whenever the display is ready. A frame that lands between two ticks would show the last tick's position, which stutters on high\-refresh displays and whenever the two rates do not divide evenly. \fBalpha\fR (0..1) says how far the simulation is toward the next tick. Draw moving things at the interpolated position:
.PP
.RS 4
.EX
static Vec2 prev_player;                       /* scene static */
\&
static void update(Engine *e, Scene *s, float dt) {
  GameState *g = e\->user;
  prev_player = g\->player.pos;                 /* first line of update */
  ... move the player ...
}
static void render(Engine *e, Scene *s, float alpha) {
  Vec2 p = v2_lerp(prev_player, g\->player.pos, alpha);
  ...
}
.EE
.RE
.PP
Keep a \fBprev\fR for everything that moves visibly (a parallel array for entity kinds) and for the camera position if it follows. Things that teleport (respawn, room change) should set \fBprev = pos\fR at the same time so they do not smear across the screen. Interpolation adds one tick of latency (under 17 ms at 60 Hz), which is imperceptible; skip it only for the few games that run update at the display rate anyway.
.PP
.SH LOGICAL PIXELS AND LAYOUT
\fBe\->logical_w\fR and \fBe\->logical_h\fR are the canvas size. Center things arithmetically: \fBx = (e\->logical_w \- w) / 2\fR. Measure text with text_size(3). Anchor HUD elements to edges (\fBe\->logical_w \- 8 \- w\fR) so a later resolution change moves them correctly. The mouse position in Input is already in logical pixels. Positions are floats and are not snapped to the logical grid: at window scale 4, a sprite at x = 10.25 lands one window pixel off the grid, which shows as uneven pixel art. Round draw positions in render (\fBfloorf(p.x)\fR) for anything that should stay on the pixel grid; keep the unrounded value in state so slow motion still accumulates.
.PP
.SH WORLD VERSUS SCREEN
World coordinates are where entities live; screen coordinates are where they are drawn. A \fBCamera\fR converts between them (gge\-camera(7)); everything in the world goes through \fBcamera_to_screen\fR / \fBcamera_rect\fR, and HUD elements do not. Games without scrolling can use world == screen and skip the camera entirely.
.PP
.SH TEXTURES AND SPRITES
\fBassets_texture\fR loads an image once and caches it (gge\-assets(7)); \fBdraw_texture\fR shows all or part of it; \fBsprite_from\fR slices a sheet into equal frames and sprite_draw(3) / sprite_draw_scaled(3) / sprite_draw_tinted(3) draw one frame with flip, rotation, scale or tint (gge\-sprite(7)). Scaling is nearest\-neighbour, so pixel art stays crisp at integer scales and looks slightly uneven at fractional ones; keep camera zoom at 1, 2, 3.
.PP
.SH TEXT
text_draw(3) draws a TrueType string at a position (gge\-text(7)); draw_debug_text is the 8x8 fallback that needs no font. Text is cached per string, so static labels are free and a per\-frame changing string costs one small texture upload.
.PP
.SH EFFECTS WITH PRIMITIVES
.TP
\fBFade to black\fR
\fBdraw_rect(full_screen, rgba(0, 0, 0, (uint8_t)(fade * 255)))\fR last, with \fBfade\fR tweened 0..1 (gge\-tween(7)).
.TP
\fBDamage flash\fR
\fBsprite_draw_tinted(&hero, f, x, y, rgb(255, 80, 80), flip)\fR for a few ticks after a hit.
.TP
\fBInvulnerability blink\fR
skip the draw when \fB(g\->ticks / 4) % 2\fR during the window.
.TP
\fBHealth / progress bar\fR
two draw_rects, the second's width scaled by the fraction.
.TP
\fBShadow\fR
a dark, half\-transparent rectangle or ellipse\-shaped sprite under the entity, drawn before it.
.TP
\fBScreen shake\fR
add the Shake offsets to the camera (gge\-tween(7), gge\-camera(7)).
.TP
\fBNight\fR
a translucent dark\-blue rectangle over the whole world, drawn after the entities and before the HUD. A light source is a soft, translucent, warm\-colored sprite drawn after that layer around the source; there is no subtractive blending, so real shadows are out of scope.
.TP
\fBParallax background\fR
draw a wide background sprite at \fB\-cam.pos.x * 0.5f\fR (world x scaled down) before the tilemap.
.TP
\fBParticles\fR
an array of pos/vel/life/color drawn with draw_rect of 1\-3 pixels or a tiny sprite; hundreds are fine.
.SH PERFORMANCE
The renderer batches consecutive draws of the same texture; thousands of sprite draws per frame are fine. Costs to know: \fBtext_draw\fR of a string that changes every frame re\-renders it; \fBtilemap_draw\fR only visits visible tiles, but a second full\-screen map doubles that; \fBdraw_point\fR per pixel across the screen is slow (use sprites). Measure with F3's fps before optimizing anything.
.PP
